<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ahupua-a">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahupuaʻa</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ahupuaa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An ahupuaʻa is a traditional Hawaiian division of land, usually a slice of an island running from the mountains to the sea and taking in one or more whole watersheds with their marine resources, so that each held a cross section of the island's resources. It ranked below the island and the moku and was divided into ʻili and kuleana farmed by commoners, who paid labour taxes to support the chief. Each was ruled by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alii">aliʻi</a> and run by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konohiki">konohiki</a>; the name joins ahu, 'heap', and puaʻa, 'pig', for the stone cairns on its boundary where offerings, often a pig, were left for the chief. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mahele">Great Mahele</a> of 1848 most were split up, but many towns keep their names.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hawaiian society rested on a hierarchy of chiefs (aliʻi), priests and commoners (makaʻāinana), bound by the religious restrictions of the kapu system and organised in land divisions, <b>ahupuaʻa</b>, that ran from the uplands to the sea, where irrigated taro, fishponds and fishing fed a dense population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Paramount chiefs ruled whole Hawaiian islands, divided into land units called <b>ahupuaʻa</b> that lesser chiefs managed for them.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>They served the ruler and the high chiefs as attendants and stewards and, above all, as konohiki, the agents who managed an <b>ahupuaʻa</b>, a land division that usually ran from the mountains to the sea.</blockquote><a href="/societies/hawaii">Social Hierarchy of Hawaii under the Kapu</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition credits him with reorganising land into <b>ahupuaʻa</b>, wedge-shaped units running from mountain to sea, and building stone temples.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a></li></ul></section>
